            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Running `:Inspect` at the position of the cursor in screenshot shows the following:\r\n\r\n```\r\nTreesitter\r\n  - @none.svelte links to Normal svelte\r\n```\r\n\r\nAs it links to `Normal` highlight group, it defines background which overrides cursorline (as this is how extmarks with background work).\r\n\r\nThis comes from 'nvim-treesitter/nvim-treesitter' and [which has the following its 'CONTRIBUTING.md'](https://github.com/nvim-treesitter/nvim-treesitter/blob/master/CONTRIBUTING.md#non-highlighting-captures): `@none    ; completely disable the highlight`. The 'mini.hues' module interprets \"completely disable\" as \"disable both foreground and background\" and does so by linking to `Normal`.\r\n\r\nThis particular use of `@none` [comes from 'svelte' queries](https://github.com/nvim-treesitter/nvim-treesitter/blob/cc2c11c403a9143827501223b4ee64432dcaf246/queries/svelte/highlights.scm#L3) and seems intentional.\r\n\r\n'folke/tokyonight.nvim' has different behavior because [it ignores the `@none` capture completely](https://github.com/folke/tokyonight.nvim/blob/efd1417aa01af618426fe1cf507c5458090458f2/lua/tokyonight/groups/treesitter.lua#L73), which doesn't align with documentation in 'nvim-treesitter/nvim-treesitter'.\r\n\r\n------\r\n\r\nIf you don't like this behavior, you can follow the Tokyonight route and add `vim.api.nvim_set_hl(0, '@none', {})` after enabling 'mini.hues' based color scheme. A more elaborate approach is to define `@none` to only have foreground from `Normal`, but that is left as an exercise in how to use `vim.api.nvim_get_hl()`.",
